<!--
  THE ONE HEAD. Every template includes this file and declares no type, colour or spacing of its own.

  WHY A PARTIAL AND NOT THREE COPIES: three templates that each carry their own <mj-attributes> agree
  on the day they are written and disagree on the second edit, because the edit lands in the file
  someone had open. There is one set of decisions here and three documents that consume it.

  IT CARRIES NO <mj-head> WRAPPER OF ITS OWN. MJML 5 refuses a nested head, so this file is the head's
  CHILDREN and each template includes it inside its own `<mj-head>` — which is also where the one head
  element that cannot be shared lives: `mj-preview`, the inbox-list line, which is per-template by
  definition.

  ══ THE SHAPE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  A letter on a near-white ground, and the letter is one padded card:

      ACCENT     a 3px rule in the accent across the very top of the card
      MASTHEAD   #03111d in BOTH themes, the logo left and the desk chip right
      CARD       #ffffff, 1px hairline, 12px corner, 32px of padding
      FOOTBAND   #edeef2, attached under the letter, who sent this and why
      GROUND     #f6f7fb, and the card floats on it

  The ground is `--bo-root-bg`, which the bridge derives as `oklch(from --bo-base-200 0.9765 c h)`
  and which resolves to `#f6f7fb`. It was NOT picked to look like the reference; it is the portal's
  own page floor, and it happening to land on the same value is why this shape costs nothing.

  A near-white ground means the card clears it by 1.07:1, so the CARD'S EDGE IS THE HAIRLINE and not
  the fill. That is DESIGN.md's own sentence about depth ("a 1px hairline and a change of surface
  tone") in the one situation where the tone alone cannot carry it. Remove the border and the card
  stops existing; that is the thing to check first if this ever looks wrong.

  ══ THE PALETTE, APPLIED FROM `brand/baseout-bridge.css` ════════════════════════════════════════

      role                    light      dark       token
      ground                  #f6f7fb    #0a0a0a    --bo-root-bg / --bo-floor
      masthead                #03111d    #03111d    --bo-primary-content (dark)
      masthead label          #a7acb0    #a7acb0    --bo-muted over the masthead
      chip border             #1f4d65    #1f4d65    --bo-primary (dark) at 32% over it
      reference box border    #89b6ca    #32647a    --bo-primary over the soft fill
      quoted block fill       #f6f7fb    #1f1f1f    --bo-root-bg / white at 6% over the card
      footer band             #edeef2    #1a1a1a    --bo-base-200
      card                    #ffffff    #111111    --bo-base-100
      hairline                #d5d7de    #27272a    --bo-base-300
      ink                     #111111    #ffffff    --bo-base-content
      muted                   #646464    #acacac    --bo-muted (65%)
      accent                  #156e97    #5cceff    --bo-primary
      accent label            #ffffff    #03111d    --bo-primary-content
      band, "copy this"       #d0e2ea    #203741    --bo-soft-primary-bg
      its label               #1a5f81    #7bd6ff    --bo-soft-primary-fg
      band, neutral           #edeef2    #1a1a1a    --bo-base-200

  EVERY VALUE IN THE LAST FOUR ROWS WAS MEASURED, NOT DERIVED BY HAND. `--bo-muted` and the soft
  pairs are `oklch()` and `color-mix()` expressions, and a mail client resolves neither, so each was
  composited in a browser over its real ground and read back as a byte. Do not recompute them in your
  head: a linear-sRGB guess lands close enough to look right and far enough to fail contrast. Every
  pair clears 4.5:1 on its own ground — light 5.25 · 5.66 · 5.92 · 13.7, dark 7.66 · 7.93 · 8.32 ·
  10.55 · 18.88.

  ══ BOTH THEMES, AND WHAT THAT CLAIM IS WORTH ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  DESIGN.md: both themes first-class and solved separately, neither a filter over the other. So the
  dark block below is a second SOLVED palette in the product's own dark tokens, not the light one
  inverted. What it is worth depends entirely on the client, and there are three kinds:

    1. HONOURS `prefers-color-scheme` — Apple Mail on macOS and iOS, Outlook for Mac and iOS,
       Superhuman, Hey, Fastmail, Thunderbird. These get exactly what is written below.
    2. INVERTS ON ITS OWN TERMS — Gmail's web client and its Android and iOS apps, Outlook.com.
       They do not read the media query. They apply their own transform, partially and
       inconsistently, and its results cannot be predicted from source. Declaring
       `color-scheme: light dark` (below, and in two `<meta>` tags) is the one lever that reaches
       them: it tells the client the message handles dark mode itself, which reduces how much
       Gmail rewrites. REDUCES. Not prevents.
    3. IGNORES ALL OF IT — Outlook on Windows, which renders through Word and reads no media query
       at all. It gets the light palette, permanently, which is correct rather than degraded.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HANDLED: Outlook.com stamps `data-ogsc` / `data-ogsb` attributes onto
  elements whose colours it has rewritten, and a third copy of every rule under `[data-ogsc]` would
  reach it. It is not here. A third copy of thirty declarations drifts from the other two, and it
  would be a claim about a client nobody in this repo can open. The readme names it as a known gap.

  ══ THE STRUCTURE IS THE POINT, AND IT CAME FROM A REFERENCE ═══════════════════════════════════
  A reference design was brought in 2026-08-24 for its STRUCTURE, explicitly not its palette. What it
  contributed, and why each piece earns its place:

    - A LEDE UNDER THE TITLE that says what the reference is FOR. The block below it used to arrive
      unexplained, and a labelled value with no sentence attached is a form field, not a receipt.
    - THE REFERENCE AS AN INSET BLOCK rather than a full-bleed band, with its caption INSIDE it. The
      band and its explanation used to be two things separated by 24px of white; one block is one
      idea. It is a CALLOUT, which the system already sanctions (Starlight's asides are a tint plus a
      border), not a card inside a card: no shadow, no elevation, and it sits in the text column
      rather than on the page floor.
    - "WHAT HAPPENS NEXT" AS THREE NUMBERED STEPS. This is the one that mattered. The same three
      facts were a single muted paragraph, which is the shape a reader skips; ordered and numbered
      they answer "what do I do now" at a glance. The words are unchanged, and steps 01 and 02 are
      still verbatim `ANSWERED_BY_EMAIL` from `lib/submit.ts` — the lockstep with the on-screen
      receipt is now word-level rather than paragraph-level, and it still has to be kept.
    - THE QUOTED BLOCK AS A FILL rather than an indent, so what the customer sent reads as an
      artefact rather than as more of our prose.
    - A REAL FOOTER BAND. See below.

  WHAT THE REFERENCE HAD AND THESE DELIBERATELY DO NOT: a thick coloured stripe down the left of both
  inset blocks, which DESIGN.md bans by name; a status badge in the masthead, because a status
  printed into a mailbox is a snapshot that goes stale and would still read CASE OPEN a month after
  the case closed; and a "check case status" button, because the acknowledgement reaches people with
  no account and no anonymous portal view exists, so the button would be a dead end for most of the
  people it is shown to. The text link stays; it is an offer rather than a promise.

  NO ICONS, AND NOT FOR WANT OF A SET. Lucide is the product's one icon set and an email cannot use
  it: an icon is an image, images are blocked by default in exactly the clients this audience reads
  mail in, and an icon that carries meaning would carry it only for some readers. Everything a glyph
  would have done is done by a numeral, a label or a rule.

  ══ THE FOOTER IS TRANSACTIONAL, WHICH DECIDES WHAT IS IN IT ═══════════════════════════════════
  All three of these are transactional: a receipt, a human's answer, and a credential. That is not a
  style question, it is what the footer is allowed to say.

    - NO UNSUBSCRIBE LINK, and adding one would be worse than leaving it out. Nobody can unsubscribe
      from the answer to their own support request; the control would either lie or break the case.
      CAN-SPAM exempts transactional and relationship messages from the opt-out requirement, and the
      `List-Unsubscribe` header must NOT be set on these either.
    - NO POSTAL ADDRESS. The address requirement is on COMMERCIAL messages; these are exempt, and an
      address invented to look complete is worse than an honest absence. If Baseout wants one it is a
      real decision with a real string, not a placeholder.
    - WHAT IS THERE INSTEAD IS "WHY YOU ARE RECEIVING THIS", naming the address and the case. That
      single line is the strongest thing a transactional footer can do: it is what stops a reader who
      does not recognise the mail from reporting it as spam, which is the outcome that actually costs
      us the channel.

  THE MOMENT BASEOUT SENDS ANYTHING NON-TRANSACTIONAL — a digest, a product update, a newsletter —
  that message needs both the unsubscribe and the address, and these three still do not. Do not
  "harmonise" the footers when that day comes.

  ══ DESIGN.md IS NOT THE AUTHORITY IN THIS DIRECTORY, AND THAT IS A RULING ═════════════════════
  Oleh, 2026-08-24, asked directly about the conflict: **ignore it**. Two reasons, both his. The
  email design is not approved yet, and it does not connect to the product UI in the first place.

  SO DO NOT "FIX" THE 4px BAR ON THE QUOTED BLOCKS. `DESIGN.md` says "don't use `border-left` or
  `border-right` greater than 1px as a coloured accent stripe", the quoted blocks here carry a 4px
  bar, and that is not a violation to be tidied up: it is a deliberate mark in a medium that document
  does not govern. The 1px version was built twice and rejected twice. This paragraph exists because
  the next person to open this file will see 4px, remember the rule, and helpfully undo it.

  WHY THE SCOPE SPLIT IS REAL AND NOT AN EXCUSE. Email is a different medium with different
  primitives: no icon set (an icon is an image and images are blocked), no webfont, no custom
  properties, no flexbox, no reliable `display`, and tables as the only dependable layout. A rule
  written for a browser surface cannot be assumed to transfer, and where it does not, the email wins
  here and the product wins there. What DOES carry across is the PALETTE, which is why every colour
  in this file is named beside the bridge token it comes from: the tokens are shared, the layout
  rules are not.

  THE CONFLICT IS STILL REAL IN THE PRODUCT'S OWN DOCUMENTATION, where the callouts ship 4px on a
  browser surface that DESIGN.md unambiguously governs. That one is not settled by this ruling and
  is logged in `audit/` as an unresolved conflict. It is not this directory's problem.

  ══ RESTRAINED, AND THE ORDER IS THE DESIGN ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Colour marks three things and no fourth: IDENTITY (the logo), what can be ACTED ON (the button,
  every link), and THE ONE STRING THE READER COPIES (a full-bleed band). The four semantic hues are
  absent because none of these three emails reports a state; when one does, it takes the soft pair
  from the bridge rather than a new colour.

  THE BAND MARKS WHAT GETS COPIED, and which band says how much it matters. The acknowledgement's
  reference is the payload, so it gets the soft-primary band. The login link's URL is the FALLBACK
  for its button, so it gets the neutral band and does not argue with it. The reply has nothing to
  copy and gets no band at all, which is also why it is the quietest of the three: a person wrote its
  payload, and an email that decorates a human's sentences reads as though they were generated.

  A BAND IS NOT A NESTED CARD. The wireframe's receipt was a filled box with a corner radius floating
  inside the white card, which DESIGN.md prohibits by name. A band has no border and no radius and
  runs edge to edge: it divides the document, the way a rule does.

  ══ THE LOGO IS ONE REAL FILE, BECAUSE ITS GROUND NEVER CHANGES ═══════════════════════════════
  `public/email/baseout-logo-cyan@2x.png`, rasterised from the brand kit's own
  `apps/web/public/images/logo/logo-dark.svg` at 240x64 and shown at 120x32.

  THE MASTHEAD IS `#03111d` IN BOTH THEMES, and that single decision deletes the hardest problem in
  dark-mode email. A logo drawn for a white card is wrong on a dark one, so a card-coloured masthead
  forces two files, a `display` swap, and an Outlook conditional to stop both showing at once. Pin
  the masthead to one dark value and the logo has one ground, one colourway and one file. The value
  is `--bo-primary-content` from the dark theme: a real token, brand-tinted rather than neutral
  black, and it carries the cyan lockup at 10.64:1.

    - PNG, NOT SVG. Gmail strips SVG; Word cannot draw it. No format works everywhere, so the choice
      is which one fails, and PNG fails only where images are blocked.
    - TRANSPARENT. Its ground is a solid `#03111d` band in every theme, so even a client that
      composites transparency onto black produces the intended picture.
    - AND THE ALT TEXT IS STYLED, which is the part people skip. Where images are blocked the alt
      renders as the wordmark in cyan at 20px on the dark band, so the masthead degrades to a typeset
      wordmark rather than to a broken-image box. Without this, an image masthead would be a defect
      for the reader most likely to be reading it.

  IN DARK, THE BAND NEEDS A HAIRLINE. `#03111d` against the dark card `#111111` measures 1.01:1, so
  the band is invisible on its own; a `#27272a` rule under it is what draws the edge. That is not a
  workaround, it is DESIGN.md's model of depth in dark ("a 1px hairline and a change of surface
  tone") in the case where the tone alone cannot carry it. In light the step is 19:1 and needs nothing.

  ══ NO WEBFONT ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Urbanist is the brand face and it is deliberately absent: a downloaded face fails silently in
  Outlook, so half the readership would see a different email from the other half, and neither half
  would know. The type ladder below is the brand's own rung set (12 · 13 · 14 · 16 · 20 · 24), which
  is what actually carries the hierarchy; the face carries less of it than it feels like it does.

  ══ LITERALS, NOT VARIABLES ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Mail clients do not run custom properties — Outlook on Windows renders through Word, which resolves
  nothing — so a `var()` here is an unstyled email. Every value above is written out twice, once per
  theme, and that duplication is the price of the second palette.
-->
<mj-raw>
  <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
  <meta name="supported-color-schemes" content="light dark" />
</mj-raw>

<mj-attributes>
  <mj-all font-family="-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif" />

  <mj-body background-color="#f6f7fb" css-class="bo-ground" />

  <mj-text color="#111111" font-size="16px" line-height="1.6" padding="0" css-class="bo-ink" />

  <!-- Sections carry no ground of their own unless they are a BAND, because the card is painted once
       by the wrapper each template opens. A section that painted itself white here would square off
       the corners it sits behind. -->
  <mj-section background-color="transparent" padding="0" />
  <mj-column padding="0" />
  <mj-wrapper background-color="#ffffff" border="1px solid #d5d7de" border-radius="12px" padding="0" css-class="bo-card" />

  <mj-divider border-width="1px" border-style="solid" border-color="#d5d7de" padding="0" css-class="bo-rule" />
  <mj-image padding="0" />

  <!-- --bo-r-8 is DESIGN.md's button shape: gently rounded, never a pill outside a filter chip.
       The inner padding is 14px rather than the product's 10px because a finger is not a cursor and
       this is the one control in the whole set; 14 + 16 + 14 clears 44px and stays on the 4px grid.
       Word drops the corner entirely and the button degrades to a teal rectangle, which is fine. -->
  <mj-button background-color="#156e97" color="#ffffff" font-size="16px" font-weight="600"
             border-radius="8px" inner-padding="14px 24px" padding="0" css-class="bo-btn" />

  <!-- The one page title. --bo-t-24, a 1.5 step over body. It deliberately does NOT go to the 32px
       display rung: the brand's density target is Linear and Vercel. -->
  <mj-class name="h1" font-size="24px" font-weight="600" line-height="1.3" letter-spacing="-0.01em" color="#111111" />

  <!-- Secondary text. --bo-t-13. -->
  <mj-class name="muted" font-size="13px" color="#646464" line-height="1.6" />

  <!-- The label rung, uppercase. The brand sets it at 11px; these use 12 because a mail client picks
       its own hinting and a rung that is legible in a browser at 11 is not reliably legible in Word.
       12 is a rung, and it is the twelve-pixel floor rather than a value below it. -->
  <mj-class name="label" font-size="12px" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="0.04em" color="#646464" line-height="1.4" />


  <!-- THE REFERENCE. The one string a person retypes, so it leads at --bo-t-20 in a monospaced face
       and is printed exactly once per email. Monospace is not decoration here: it is what makes the
       difference between an O and a 0 visible to someone copying it into a subject line by hand. -->
  <mj-class name="ref" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace" font-size="20px" letter-spacing="0.02em" color="#111111" line-height="1.4" />

  <!-- The reference again, at the reply's rank rather than the acknowledgement's. Same monospaced
       face for the same reason: whatever size it is set at, this string gets retyped, and an O that
       cannot be told from a 0 is a case number that threads to nothing. -->
  <mj-class name="refsm" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace" font-size="13px" letter-spacing="0.02em" color="#646464" line-height="1.4" />

  <!-- Quoted material: what the sender wrote, and the thread so far. Muted and one rung down, set
       apart by a rule and an indent rather than by going fainter still — history a reader cannot
       make out is not included, it is decoration. -->
  <mj-class name="quote" font-size="13px" color="#646464" line-height="1.6" />

  <!-- THE EYEBROW. The label rung in the accent, above the title. It is not the title restated: it
       names the KIND of mail, which a reader scanning three Baseout messages in one thread needs and
       the sentence below it does not give. Where a real identifier exists the eyebrow carries that
       instead of a category, because a case reference tells the reader more than the word "reply". -->
  <mj-class name="eyebrow" font-size="12px" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="0.08em" color="#156e97" line-height="1.4" />

  <!-- The desk name, right of the logo on the masthead. Baseout will send product mail too, and this
       says which desk this one came from. --bo-muted resolved against the masthead, 8.32:1. -->
  <mj-class name="mastlabel" font-size="12px" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="0.08em" color="#a7acb0" line-height="1.4" />

  <!-- THE STEP NUMERAL. Monospaced so 01 · 02 · 03 hold one column width without a table trick, and
       in the accent because the numeral is the only thing marking the list as ordered: an email
       cannot use a Lucide glyph (an icon is an image, and images are blocked exactly where this
       matters most), so the numeral carries what a glyph would carry in the product. -->
  <mj-class name="step" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace" font-size="13px" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="0.04em" color="#156e97" line-height="1.6" />

  <!-- The footer, which lives OUTSIDE the card on the ground. Same muted rung; it reads as the
       envelope's own small print rather than as the last paragraph of the letter. -->
  <mj-class name="foot" font-size="13px" color="#646464" line-height="1.6" />
</mj-attributes>

<mj-style>
  /* Declared in CSS as well as in the two meta tags above, because clients read one or the other and
     no client reads both reliably. This is what tells a client the message handles dark mode itself. */
  :root { color-scheme: light dark; supported-color-schemes: light dark; }

  /* Links take the accent AND keep the underline. The underline is not a wireframe leftover: an
     email is read in a client whose palette we do not control, and colour must never be the sole
     carrier of an affordance. That is the product's Never-Alone rule, and it applies hardest here.

     EVERY ANCHOR ALSO CARRIES THE COLOUR INLINE, and this rule is not redundant with that: Outlook
     renders through Word, which applies its own hyperlink style and does not reliably let a head
     stylesheet beat it, so a link styled only here comes out Word-blue on the client this audience
     is most likely to be reading in. The rule covers everything else; the inline attribute covers
     Word; the dark block below has to beat the inline attribute, which is what its !important is
     for. Three layers, and each one is there because the layer under it has a hole. */
  a { color: #156e97; text-decoration: underline; }

  /* THE LOGO SWAP IS BACK, and it is the direct cost of removing the dark masthead from style one.
     While the masthead was pinned to one dark value the logo had one ground and one file; with the
     logo on the card, the ground is white in light and #111111 in dark, and the ink lockup measures
     2.6:1 on the dark one. The light file is the default, so a client that reads no media query gets
     the correct image for the light card it is also going to render, and the dark one is hidden from
     Word by a conditional rather than by CSS because Word's support for `display: none` from a head
     stylesheet is not something to bet a masthead on. */
  .bo-logo-dark { display: none; }

  /* A `<td>` WITH A RADIUS DRAWS SQUARE UNDER `border-collapse: collapse`, WHICH IS WHAT MJML EMITS.
     Measured 2026-08-24: style two's boxes carried `border-radius: 8px` on the cell, the rule
     reported as applied, and every corner rendered sharp. The spec is explicit — a collapsed table
     ignores cell radii — so the radius was never wrong, the table around it was. `!important` because
     MJML writes the collapse inline, and `border-spacing: 0` because `separate` would otherwise open
     a gap between the bar and the fill it is meant to touch. */
  table.bo-round, .bo-round table {
    border-collapse: separate !important;
    border-spacing: 0 !important;
  }

  /* Long references, ids and URLs must wrap rather than force a horizontal scroll on a phone. */
  .bo-break { word-break: break-word; }

  /* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     THE DARK PALETTE. Solved separately in the product's own dark tokens, never the light palette
     inverted. Every declaration is !important because MJML writes the light value inline on the same
     element, and an inline style beats a head rule at every specificity a stylesheet can reach.

     The selector lists are doubled (`.x` and `.x div`, `.x td`) on purpose: MJML puts `css-class` on
     the outermost element it emits for a component, and which element that is differs by component.
     Naming both is cheaper than being wrong on one of them and finding out in somebody's inbox.
     ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    body, .bo-ground, .bo-ground > div { background-color: #0a0a0a !important; }

    .bo-card, .bo-card > table, .bo-card > div { background-color: #111111 !important; }
    /* THE CARD'S BORDER IS NOT ON THE ELEMENT CARRYING THE CLASS. MJML emits the wrapper as
       `div.bo-card > table > tbody > tr > td` and puts the border AND the radius on that innermost
       td, so a rule on `.bo-card` alone measures as applied and leaves a light hairline drawn round
       a dark card. Measured before this line existed: `#d5d7de` on a `#111111` card. */
    .bo-card > table > tbody > tr > td { border-color: #27272a !important; }

    .bo-ink, .bo-ink div, .bo-ink td { color: #ffffff !important; }
    .bo-muted, .bo-muted div, .bo-muted td,
    .bo-foot, .bo-foot div, .bo-foot td { color: #acacac !important; }

    .bo-rule p, p.bo-rule { border-top-color: #27272a !important; }

    .bo-indent, .bo-indent td, .bo-indent > div { border-left-color: #27272a !important; }

    .bo-kv th { color: #acacac !important; border-bottom-color: #27272a !important; }
    .bo-kv td { color: #ffffff !important; border-bottom-color: #27272a !important; }

    .bo-eyebrow, .bo-eyebrow div, .bo-eyebrow td { color: #7bd6ff !important; }

    .bo-step, .bo-step div, .bo-step td { color: #5cceff !important; }

    /* STYLE TWO ONLY. The corner brackets around the reference, the pale header strip on a labelled
       box, and the dotted rule. Each is the dark half of the token the light rule uses; none of them
       exists in style one, and a class that matches nothing costs nothing. */
    .bo-bracket, .bo-bracket td { border-color: #5cceff !important; }
    .bo-stripe, .bo-stripe td { background-color: #1a1a1a !important; }
    .bo-dots p, p.bo-dots { border-top-color: #27272a !important; }
    .bo-node-done, .bo-node-done td, .bo-node-done div { color: #5cceff !important; }
    .bo-node-todo, .bo-node-todo td, .bo-node-todo div { color: #acacac !important; }
    .bo-connector { background-color: #27272a !important; }

    /* THE QUOTE BLOCK: a 4px bar and a fill. See the ruling in the header comment. */
    .bo-qbar, td.bo-qbar { background-color: #32647a !important; }
    .bo-qbody, td.bo-qbody { background-color: #1f1f1f !important; }

    /* Style one has no masthead any more, so its logo sits on the card and the card changes colour
       with the theme. That is the swap the pinned masthead used to make unnecessary. */
    .bo-logo-light { display: none !important; }
    .bo-logo-dark { display: block !important; }

    .bo-quotebox, .bo-quotebox td, .bo-quotebox > div { background-color: #1f1f1f !important; border-color: #27272a !important; }

    .bo-footband, .bo-footband > table, .bo-footband > div { background-color: #1a1a1a !important; }

    .bo-band-ref, .bo-band-ref > table, .bo-band-ref > div { background-color: #203741 !important; }
    .bo-band-neutral, .bo-band-neutral > table, .bo-band-neutral > div { background-color: #1a1a1a !important; }

    a, .bo-ink a, .bo-muted a, .bo-foot a { color: #5cceff !important; }

    .bo-btn a, .bo-btn table, a.bo-btn { background-color: #5cceff !important; color: #03111d !important; }

  }
</mj-style>
